title: Store tables
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The key-value store's tables, primary and derived, and how their keys are built.

The store is a set of LMDB tables, each an ''ordered map of byte strings'': a read is
a lookup or a scan of a key range. Everything below is designed around that — keys
are built so that what a read needs together is one contiguous range.

!! How keys are built

* Integers are big-endian, so byte order is numeric order.
* A uuid is its 16 bytes.
* A name inside a composite key (a field name, a node's name) is escaped (`00` → `00 FF`) and ended by `00 00`, so that no name is a prefix of another: `tag` and `tags` never share a range.
* `·` below separates the parts of a composite key.

!! Primary tables (`kvstore/mod.rs`)

What was written. The rest of the daemon reaches it through `Rows` and `Log`
([[The storage boundary]]).

|!Table |!Key |!Value |!Serves |
|`meta` |name |the id counters, HEAD, the layout and derived-format versions, the watcher's label | |
|`metarecords` |uuid |version |existence, [[Metarecord version]] |
|`field_cells` |uuid · field name · row id |field name · value |the rows themselves |
|`row_owner` |row id |uuid · field name |a row by its id alone (`…/fields/:id`) |
|`by_field` |field name · row id |uuid |every row, every holder, of one field |
|`field_types` |field name · value type |how many rows of that type |a field's type ([[One value type per field name]]) |
|`forest` |field · parent · name bytes |uuid · row id |a child by name: path lookup |
|`ops` |op id |the operation |the log, in order ([[The operation log]]) |
|`snaps` |op id · after? · index |a snapshot row |what an operation changed, before and after |
|`ops_by_label` |label · op id |— |an undo or revert by label |
|`ops_by_entity` |uuid · op id |— |a metarecord's history |
|`labels` |label id |name · description · parent · created |[[Labels and contexts]] |
|`label_kids` |parent label · child label |— |the labels' nesting |
|`label_keys` |command key |label id |a command finding its label at its next write |
|`reverted_by` |op id · op id of a revert of it |— |whether an operation still stands ([[The undo selection]]) |
|`watcher_ops`, `user_ops` |op id |— |the two retention classes ([[Automatic retention]]) |

A record's rows ''of one field'' are one range of `field_cells`, so checking a
candidate on the field searched reads those rows, not all of its record's — a real
file carries a dozen `mfr_*` and metadata fields besides. Row, operation and label ids
come from counters in `meta` and are never reused; a row put back under its own id
(a restore) moves the counter past it.

!! Derived tables — the indexes (`kvstore/derived.rs`)

Computed from the primary tables, read by the query evaluator through `KvSource`.

|!Table |!Key |!Value |
|`ids` |uuid |dense id (u32) |
|`uuids` |dense id |uuid |
|`sets` |kind · [field] · [key] · chunk |a roaring bitmap of dense ids |
|`parts` |field · partition · key · dense id |rows (count) |
|`kids` |parent uuid · field · dense id |rows (count) |
|`grams` |field · trigram · chunk |a roaring bitmap of dense ids |

''Dense ids'' come first: each metarecord gets a small integer, allocated in
discovery order, never renumbered, never reused. Bitmaps hold these ids, not uuids.
The files of one folder mostly get consecutive ids, so a folder's bitmaps compress to
a few runs.

A ''set'' is a bitmap cut in chunks of 65 536 ids (the high 16 bits), one key per
chunk: a write rewrites one chunk, a read unions a few. The kinds:

|!Kind |!Keyed by |!Holds |
|universe |— |every metarecord |
|present |field |the ids holding a value of the field |
|absent |field |the ids holding `Nothing` in it |
|parents |field |on a `tree_ref` field, the ids with a child |
|referrers |field · target |the ids pointing at a uuid — a folder's children, a `ref`'s referrers ([[The forest in the store]]) |
|descendants |field · node |every id below a node ([[The forest in the store]]) |
|postings |field · value key |the holders of a frequent value ([[Sorting and postings]]) |
|long texts |field |the ids whose text is too long for trigrams ([[Text search in the store]]) |
|multi |field |the ids holding several rows of the field, whose sort representative a walk must read one by one |

A ''partition'' (`parts`) lists a field's rows in the order of a key whose bytes sort
like the values, so equality, ranges and sorts are range scans. Three per field:
''value'' (the value itself), ''name'' (a `tree_ref`'s name), ''target'' (the uuid a
value points at — its referent, or its parent).

`kids` is the forest keyed by parent first, so a metarecord arriving or leaving finds
the fields it is a parent in without a scan. `grams` holds one bitmap per trigram of a
field's texts ([[Text search in the store]]).

Every row change goes through one primitive (`derive_row`), every metarecord creation
and removal through two others (`derive_created`, `derive_removed`) — reverts
included —, so the indexes are maintained in each write's own transaction; `mf repo
check` holds all of them to a rebuild.

!! Key size

LMDB refuses a key over 511 bytes, and a partition key (holding a value) or a forest
key (holding a name) can hold any text:

* ''A text over 256 escaped bytes'' is keyed by that prefix, a marker `00 01` and a 64-bit hash of the whole. The marker sorts after the `00 00` ending a text equal to the prefix and before anything longer, so order is kept — except among long texts sharing the prefix, which a reader resolves from the values.
* ''A node name over 300 bytes'' is keyed by its first 300 bytes and a hash, and read back from its row.
* ''A field name'' is part of every key: one over about 200 bytes cannot be written.
